%DocsPage
  %PageHeader{
    kicker: "Plugin Reference",
    title: "JavaScriptPlugin",
    summary: "JavaScriptPlugin emits content-hashed JavaScript assets, rewrites module imports, resolves npm packages, supports TypeScript and TSX, and can expose TSX custom elements to Ruby and Haml."
  }

  %DocsSection{ title: "Source Files" }
    :markdown
      `JavaScriptPlugin` handles `.js`, `.jsx`, `.ts`, and `.tsx` files. Ruby and Haml imports receive either the emitted JavaScript asset path or, for JSX and TSX custom element modules, a custom element descriptor.

      ```ruby
      Demo = import("./demo.js")
      TimeDemo = import("./TimeDemo.tsx")
      ```

      TypeScript and JSX transforms are provided by the native extension, which uses SWC. Plain JavaScript can still be scanned without the native extension, but TypeScript and JSX require it.

  %DocsSection{ title: "JavaScript Imports" }
    :markdown
      Static imports, re-exports, and literal dynamic imports are resolved through the Klenod graph and rewritten to content-hashed browser asset paths.

      ```js
      import message from "./message";
      export { value } from "./value.js";
      button.addEventListener("click", () => import("./Panel.js"));
      ```

      Relative imports, app-root imports, `npm://` imports, and external URL imports are supported. Extensionless JavaScript imports resolve according to the importer: TSX prefers `.tsx`, `.ts`, `.jsx`, then `.js`; TS prefers `.ts`, then `.js`.

  %DocsSection{ title: "npm Packages" }
    :markdown
      Bare specifiers stay relative to the importer, so npm packages are addressed through the `npm://` scheme. Resolution is handled by `NodeModulesPlugin`, which is a separate plugin and must be registered before `JavaScriptPlugin`.

      ```ruby
      plugins [
        Klenod::Build::Plugins::NodeModulesPlugin.new,
        Klenod::Build::Plugins::JavaScriptPlugin.new
      ]
      ```

      A specifier is `npm://` followed by the package name, optionally followed by a subpath. Scoped packages keep their `@scope/name` spelling.

      ```js
      import * as THREE from "npm://three";
      import { vec3 } from "npm://gl-matrix";
      import { OrbitControls } from "npm://three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js";
      import { ReactiveElement } from "npm://@lit/reactive-element";
      import { css } from "npm://@lit/reactive-element/css-tag.js";
      ```

      Side-effect imports, re-exports, and dynamic imports all work, and resolve to the same content-hashed asset as a static import of the same module.

      ```js
      import "npm://@scope/widget";
      export { html } from "npm://lit";
      button.addEventListener("click", () => import("npm://three"));
      ```

      Whether a subpath needs a file extension depends on the package, because a subpath has to match what that package's `exports` field declares. `three` publishes `./addons/*` and expects `npm://three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js`, while `zod` publishes `./mini` and expects `npm://zod/mini`.

      Package files go through the graph like any other module, so they are transformed, content-hashed, minified in build mode, and shared when two modules import the same package.

  %DocsSection{ title: "npm Resolution" }
    :markdown
      A package entry resolves through its `exports` field, matched against the `browser`, `import`, `module`, and `default` conditions. Conditions are matched in the order the package lists them rather than the order above, which is how Node and browser bundlers behave. A package with no `exports` field falls back to `module`, then `main`, then `index.js`.

      Inside a package, bare specifiers name other packages and resolve by walking up through `node_modules`, nearest first, so a nested copy takes precedence over a hoisted one. A package that declares `exports` can also import itself by name, which is how libraries such as `three` reference their own core from bundled addons. Imports that omit an extension are probed for `.js` and `.mjs`, then for an `index.js` or `index.mjs` inside a directory. Targets named by `exports` are never probed, since that field names exact files. A package can also use `#`-prefixed private imports, resolved against its own `imports` field.

      Only ES modules are supported. The `require` condition is deliberately not matched, so a CommonJS-only package fails when it is resolved rather than in the browser. Node builtins are reported the same way: a package that imports `node:fs`, or `fs` with nothing installed under that name, fails at resolve time.

      Module IDs are `npm://<package>/<path>`, so a package name identifies one directory. Finding two copies of the same package is reported as an error rather than resolved, because the ID has nowhere to record which copy a module came from.

      `NodeModulesPlugin.new(root:)` overrides the `node_modules` directory, which otherwise defaults to the nearest one at or above the source directory. `NodeModulesPlugin.new(conditions:)` overrides the matched export conditions.

  %DocsSection{ title: "CSS And Asset Imports" }
    :markdown
      JavaScript can import CSS directly. CSS imports are rewritten to native browser CSS module imports using `with { type: "css" }`, and the referenced stylesheet is emitted as an unscoped CSS asset.

      ```ts
      import styles from "./TimeDemo.css";

      this.shadowRoot.adoptedStyleSheets = [styles];
      ```

      Imported CSS assets are added to JavaScript preload metadata so framework servers can send early hints and `Link` headers.

      Raster image and SVG imports are rewritten to small JavaScript metadata modules:

      ```js
      import photo from "./photo.jpg";
      import logo from "./logo.svg";
      ```

      Wrappers import a shared asset metadata module. `ImageMetadata` and `ImageVariant` extend `ImageBase`, which exposes `src`, `width`, `height`, `contentType`, and a build-time `aspectRatio`. The generated metadata objects and raster variants array are frozen. SVG imports default-export an `SvgMetadata` instance whose dimensions come from numeric `width` and `height` attributes or fall back to `viewBox`; dimensions and `aspectRatio` are `null` when the SVG has no intrinsic size. Raster `srcset` and `sizes` are computed by `ImageMetadata`.

      ```js
      import ImageMetadata, { ImageVariant } from "https://cdn.klenod.dev/klenod_asset_metadata.<hash>.js";

      export default new ImageMetadata({
        src: "https://cdn.klenod.dev/photo.<hash>.jpg",
        width: 1448,
        height: 1086,
        contentType: "image/jpeg",
        aspectRatio: 1.3333333333333333,
        variants: [new ImageVariant({
          width: 640,
          height: 480,
          aspectRatio: 1.3333333333333333
        })]
      });
      ```

      These JavaScript metadata modules are emitted only when JavaScript imports the image or SVG. Ruby, Haml, and CSS-only asset imports emit the underlying browser assets without unused `.js` wrappers.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Custom Elements" }
    :markdown
      `.jsx` and `.tsx` modules are treated as custom element modules. They should default-export a named class or identifier. The plugin generates a stable custom element tag name, registers the element, and returns a descriptor when imported from Ruby or Haml.

      ```tsx
      export default class TimeDemoElement extends HTMLElement {
        connectedCallback(): void {
          this.replaceChildren(<p>Hello</p>);
        }
      }
      ```

      Haml can render the imported descriptor as a component-style tag:

      ```haml
      :ruby
        TimeDemo = import("./TimeDemo.tsx")

      %TimeDemo{ time: Time.now }
      ```

      The JSX runtime helper is provided as a virtual module and injects `h` and `Fragment` for JSX and TSX output.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Source Maps And Minification" }
    :markdown
      JavaScript assets can emit source maps in development. Build mode disables source map assets by default and always minifies JavaScript output. Development output is readable by default, but `JavaScriptPlugin.new(minify: true)` can minify development output when an app wants to test production-like assets locally.

